==================================================================
DAY 6 - PART B: LAB
Watch the request flow happen
Time: 90 minutes
==================================================================

STEP 1: Open a watcher in a second terminal
-------------------------------------------
Terminal 2:
  k get pods -w

Leave it running for the whole lab.


STEP 2: Create a pod and watch the states
-----------------------------------------
Terminal 1:
  k run web --image=nginx

In terminal 2 you should see:
  web   0/1   Pending             -> stored, not yet scheduled
  web   0/1   ContainerCreating   -> scheduled, kubelet pulling image
  web   1/1   Running


STEP 3: Read the story in events
--------------------------------
k describe pod web

Scroll to "Events" at the bottom. Match each line to the 8 steps:
  Scheduled   default-scheduler   Successfully assigned default/web to lab-worker
  Pulling     kubelet             Pulling image "nginx"
  Pulled      kubelet             Successfully pulled image
  Created     kubelet             Created container web
  Started     kubelet             Started container web

Notice WHO reports each event (scheduler vs kubelet).

k get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp


STEP 4: Find where it landed and its IP
---------------------------------------
k get pod web -o wide

Note the NODE and IP columns.


STEP 5: See the raw API calls kubectl makes
-------------------------------------------
k run web2 --image=nginx -v=6

Look for lines like:
  POST https://127.0.0.1:xxxxx/api/v1/namespaces/default/pods 201 Created

Try "k get pods -v=8" to also see the response body.


STEP 6: View the full object (spec vs status)
---------------------------------------------
k get pod web -o yaml | less

Find:
  spec.nodeName                 (written by the scheduler)
  spec.containers[0].image      (written by you)
  status.phase                  (written by the kubelet)
  status.podIP
  status.containerStatuses

Show only some fields:
  k get pod web -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}{"\n"}{.status.podIP}{"\n"}'


STEP 7: Logs and exec
---------------------
k logs web
k exec -it web -- bash
  curl -s localhost | head -5
  head /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
  hostname          # same as the pod name
  exit


STEP 8: Reach the pod from your machine
---------------------------------------
k port-forward pod/web 8080:80

Terminal 3:
  curl localhost:8080

Then check the logs:
  k logs web --tail=3

Stop port-forward with Ctrl+C.


STEP 9: Look at it from the node's side
---------------------------------------
NODE=$(k get pod web -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}')
echo $NODE
docker exec $NODE crictl ps
docker exec $NODE crictl pods

You see the nginx container running under containerd on that node,
plus a "pause" container that holds the pod's network namespace.
Same process + namespaces story as Day 2:

docker exec $NODE ps aux | grep "nginx: master"


STEP 10: Delete and watch
-------------------------
k delete pod web
k get pods

The pod is gone and NOTHING brings it back. No controller manages a
bare pod. (Deployments fix this in Week 2.)


STEP 11: Cause and read a failure
---------------------------------
k run broken --image=nginx:doesnotexist
k get pods                # ErrImagePull, then ImagePullBackOff
k describe pod broken     # read Events: "manifest unknown" / "not found"

Fix it without deleting the pod:
  k set image pod/broken broken=nginx:latest
  k get pods -w           # becomes Running

Clean up:
  k delete pod broken web2


STEP 12: Save your notes
------------------------
In Part-C write the 8 steps from memory, then commit:
  cd ~/k8s-journey && git add . && git commit -m "day6: request flow"
